# /etc/cron.d/trustissues-backup - the fallback for hosts without systemd.
#
# Install:
#   install -m 0644 deploy/cron/trustissues-backup.cron /etc/cron.d/trustissues-backup
#
# Prefer the systemd units in deploy/systemd/ when the host has systemd. They
# give you Persistent=true (a backup missed while the box was off runs at the
# next boot instead of vanishing), a sandbox, and a real failure hook. This file
# exists so a host without systemd is not left with "run backup.sh from cron"
# as a doc line, which is what DEFERRED.md section (d) called out.
#
# Three cron traps are handled explicitly below, because each of them silently
# produces a schedule that looks installed and never works:
#
#   1. cron's PATH is not your PATH. It is typically /usr/bin:/bin only, so
#      sqlite3 is frequently not found and backup.sh exits 1 every night.
#   2. cron mails output to the crontab owner ONLY if the box runs an MTA. Most
#      do not, so cron's own error reporting goes to a mail spool nobody reads,
#      or nowhere. That is why every line below ends in an explicit `|| alert`
#      instead of relying on MAILTO.
#   3. A percent sign in a cron command is a newline, not a percent. There are
#      none here. If you add a `date +%F`, escape it as `\%F` or it will
#      truncate the command at that point and you will not be told.
#
# Requires /etc/trustissues/backup.env, the same file the systemd units read.
# Copy it from deploy/systemd/backup.env.example and chmod 0600.

PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
SHELL=/bin/bash

# Daily backup + retention prune at 03:20, matching trustissues-backup.timer.
20 3 * * * root set -a; . /etc/trustissues/backup.env; set +a; export TRUSTISSUES_BACKUP_PRUNE=1 TRUSTISSUES_ALERT_LOG=/var/log/trustissues-backup.log; /opt/trustissues/scripts/backup.sh >>/var/log/trustissues-backup.log 2>&1 || /opt/trustissues/deploy/systemd/trustissues-backup-alert.sh trustissues-backup.service >>/var/log/trustissues-backup.log 2>&1

# Weekly restore drill on Mondays at 04:10, matching trustissues-restore-drill.timer.
# This is the line people leave out. Do not leave it out: the backup job above
# proves a file was written, and only this proves the file can be restored.
10 4 * * 1 root set -a; . /etc/trustissues/backup.env; set +a; export TRUSTISSUES_ALERT_LOG=/var/log/trustissues-drill.log; /opt/trustissues/scripts/restore-drill.sh >>/var/log/trustissues-drill.log 2>&1 || /opt/trustissues/deploy/systemd/trustissues-backup-alert.sh trustissues-restore-drill.service >>/var/log/trustissues-drill.log 2>&1
